<!-- 4. MANUFACTURING STANDARDS AND REGULATORY COMPLIANCE -->
<section class="section" id="4-manufacturing-standards-and-regulatory-compliance">
  <h2>4. Manufacturing Standards and Regulatory Compliance</h2>
  <p>Automotive aerosol manufacturing is subject to strict environmental, safety, and transport requirements. Facilities handling flammable propellants like LPG must operate under explosion-proof conditions and deploy automated leak detectors. Label declarations must clearly state flammability hazards, VOC levels, and disposal protocols. The paragraphs and bullets below cover the core regulatory frameworks.</p>
  <p>
                    Manufacturing automotive aerosols is a high-stakes endeavor that requires strict adherence to safety and quality protocols. Unlike standard liquid bottling, aerosol filling involves handling pressurized gases and highly flammable liquids, necessitating specialized facilities with explosion-proof equipment and advanced ventilation systems. <a href="https://www.nfpa.org">NFPA</a> 30B, covered in our <a href="/nfpa-30b-aerosol-warehouse-storage-classification/">NFPA 30B storage classification guide</a>, sets fire protection and storage requirements for facilities that manufacture, store, or display aerosol products. Compliance with Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) is not just a regulatory hurdle; it is a foundation for product reliability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Regulatory frameworks vary by region but share three themes: flammability labeling, Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) limits, and pressure vessel safety standards. In California, <a href="https://ww2.arb.ca.gov/our-work/programs/consumer-products-program/about">CARB's consumer products program</a> sets VOC limits by product category, and our guide to <a href="/carb-aerosol-voc-regulations-reformulation/">CARB aerosol VOC regulations</a> covers reformulation strategies. Transport follows hazmat rules such as <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49">49 CFR</a>, summarized in our guide to <a href="/dot-un-aerosol-transport-regulations-hazmat/">DOT and UN aerosol transport regulations</a>. Hazard information travels in the SDS format that <a href="https://www.osha.gov/hazcom">OSHA's Hazard Communication Standard</a> prescribes. In the global market, meeting these standards is a prerequisite for export and authority. Startups must invest in robust quality control systems that test every batch for pressure stability, leakage, and spray performance to ensure consumer safety and brand integrity.
                </p>
                
